on**\n\nAfter careful consideration, B1 seems to be the best move. B1 solidifies the edge near my secured corner at A1, flipping C2 to secure that area of the board. The impact is significant: B1 gives a stable wall on the left edge. I make the C2 corner into a corner for myself. B1 doesn\'t open up opponent plays. B1 extends my territory and limits the opponents\' space. This quiet move is far more valuable than opening up the game with loud flips and open edge plays.\n\n**Final Decision**\n\n(B, 1) is the strategic optimal move.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: The move (B, 1) is a strategically superior move that prioritizes stability and territory consolidation.\nReason: You currently own the entire top row (Row A), which serves as a stable base. Playing at (B, 1) anchors off the secure corner at A1. It flips the opponent's pieces at C1, B2, and C2. Flipping C1 is particularly valuable as it connects A1 to D1 (which is already yours), creating a solid, unbreakable wall of your pieces along the top-left edge (Column 1). This move maximizes stability, reduces the opponent's mobility in that sector, and does not open up any dangerous corners or edges to the opponent.\n\nAnswer: (B, 1)"}
